-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE

     VME bus driver VMEHB for Linux on PC's announced 

Natalia Kruszynska from NIKHEF Holland ( Institute of Nuclear 
and High Enenrgy Physics) announces the  VME bus available on 
Linux PC's equipped with the Model 617 PCI/VME adaptor produced 
by Bit3 Computer Corporation (TM). 

For this device the Linux driver vmehb is written, tested and 
distributed to the benefit of Linux community under GNU access.
The driver was demonstrated on the BOF session "Non-system busses 
on PC under Linux" on the last Linux Congress 22-23 May 97 in 
Wurzburg, Germany. 

In the design I was trying to follow the lines estabilished by SUN
in the times when VME was used as a system bus (Sun3), by creating
6 base VME spaces towards which the user can mmap and not worry about 
the AM or swap features or another details. These detais are to be
estabilished once by the super user VME guru and not changed often.
This practice have been benficial for the use of VME slow control
in our Institute. In practice we do not use read/write access at all.

The base access via 6 nodes in estabilished swap/am was extended
to cover the possibilities of the device ( see vmehb_conf.h ).

The device offers DMA feature, working ~10 times quicker than the
mmapped access.  This access is implemented with lseek read/write 
feature.  Lseek estabilishes the current offset ( start address) over 
the node. Read/write accesses the VME space from this offset , doing
the choice between memory mapped (short) and DMA ( longer) access.

Alas, during the DMA no other activity is allowed along the adaptor 
cable. It means that the mmap users have to be isolated from
the access of the adaptor cable during DMA.  If that is done, the
mmap users read the last value and write nothing during their
discoupling. Moreover mapping back makes the performance of the
driver slower. Thus the compromis tools are made and left in the 
hands of the super user. He can choose , whether the driver is 
to be used for the slow controls, designing the use on base of mmap 
system call or for the data acquisition. In the second  case mmap is 
not allowed.  The super user can decide whether to unmap the slow 
control window ( the unmapped job will read the last, write to no 
effect ) or to kill all the active jobs which have already mapped to 
the nodes. Extra check mode can be set by the super user , whenever 
the VME addressing errors occur. Otherwise the rough position of the 
VME error is reported .

>From the file_operations the following are included. Open and release
give node access to the user.Lseek was used to cover 32 bits VME 
addressing (not needed in 2.1.x).  Read/write use the DMA above the 
configured minimal DMA length of node and does it's own mapping for 
the short access. Mmap is allowed in slow control mode, as the most 
useful tool for this purpose.  The ioctl repertoire covers the tools 
for the super user to estabilish the driver's mode, to resolve problem 
with mmap /DMA conflict to switch the crate on/off, the tools for the 
user to check in which mode the driver works what parameters are 
set on his node and whether the crate os on or off.

The driver is serialized for the node access with the use of MUTEX
semaphore.  Thus it is not recommended to use it for massive 
multi-use under the systems lower than 2.0.27.

The distribution is in the file vmehb-1.0.tgz and contains all the 
sources, Makefile ,README.vmehb installation manual and vmehb.8 
unix/linux style manual and base test program.It also contains nice 
VME hanoi towers, which you should fix to the VME devices you 
can supply for tests.

