	C-INTERCAL 0.16

The INTERCAL programming language marked its silver anniversary on 26
May 1997. To "celebrate" this occasion, a new version of C-INTERCAL,
the Unix-based implementation of INTERCAL, has been released.

INTERCAL was originally created and implemented by Don R. Woods and
Jim Lyon. The C-INTERCAL compiler was written by Eric S. Raymond, who
continues to maintain it along with Louis Howell and "others".

The distribution includes full documentation and most known INTERCAL
programs in existence.

As of version 0.13, the C-INTERCAL compiler has been ported to Linux.
It compiles INTERCAL into C code, so a C compiler is required.
Building the C-INTERCAL compiler also requires lex/flex and
yacc/bison.

The C-INTERCAL 0.16 distribution is available via anonymous FTP at
ftp://ftp.ccil.org/pub/esr/intercal-0.16.tar.gz. It can also be
downloaded from http://www.ccil.org/~esr/intercal-0.16.tar.gz.

	new features of 0.16:

* The compiler no longer imposes an arbitrary limit on program size or
  variable usage.
* The optimizer now recognizes "several" INTERCAL idioms for native C
  operations.
* The standard library has been ported to all of the non-binary
  dialects of C-INTERCAL.
* A library of floating-point "functions" has been added to the
  collection of INTERCAL programs.
* Various bug fixes and manual corrections have been made.

	other resources

Eric S. Raymond maintains a web page about INTERCAL at:
http://www.ccil.org/~esr/intercal/.

The manual can be downloaded separately, in Postscript:
http://www.ccil.org/~esr/intercal/intercal.ps.gz; or in ASCII:
http://www.ccil.org/~esr/intercal/intercal.txt.gz. An HTML version of
the manual is at http://www.muppetlabs.com/~breadbox/intercal-man/.

alt.lang.intercal is a newsgroup devoted to the "appreciation" and
"advancement" of the "language".

	intercal.lsm

Begin3
Title:		intercal
Version:	0.16
Entered-date:	26MAY97
Description:	An implementation of the language INTERCAL, legendary for
		its perversity and horribleness (this version adds COME FROM
		for extra flavor).  Comes with language manual and examples
		including possibly the entire extant body of INTERCAL code.
Keywords:	language, compiler, retrocomputing, geek humor
Author: 	esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Maintained-by:	esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
