-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


ftape is a floppy tape device driver for Linux.

Version 3.03a doesn't introduce new features, but is merely a bug-fix
release.

Introduction
============
It compiles and works with Linux-2.0.30 and Linux-2.1.39 (maybe also
with other kernels, including 1.2.13 but that is untested)

ftape-3.x developed as the reunification of the stock ftape-2.x driver
written by Bas Laarhoven and the enhancements/modifications I had
implemented in a separate development tree called "zftape".

ftape-3.03a is implemented as a stack of kernel modules:

ftape.o  - hardware driver
zftape.o - VFS frontend
sftape.o - alternate VFS frontend, resembling the old ftape-2.x
           implementation
ftape-format.o - formatting of floppy tape cartridges

ftape-3.03a fixes the following bugs (compared with ftape-3.03)
===============================================================
- - Finally fixed auto-un-loading of modules for kernels > 2.1.18
- - Add an "uninstall" target to the Makefile
- - removed the kdtime hack
- - texi2www didn't properly set the back-reference from a footnote back
  to the regular text.

  zftape specific
  ---------------
  * hide the old compression map volume. Taper doesn't accept the
    presence of non-Taper volumes and Taper-written volume on the same
    tape.
  * EOD (End Of Data) handling was still broken: the expected behavior
    is to return a zero byte count at the first attempt to read past
    EOD, return a zero byte count at the second attempt to read past
    EOD and THEN return -EIO.
  
  ftape-format specific
  ---------------------
  * Detection of QIC-40 cartridges in select_tape_format() was broken
    and made it impossible to format QIC-3010/3020 cartridges.
  * There are strange "TR-1 Extra" cartridges out there which weren't
    detected properly because the don't strictly conform to the
    QIC-80, Rev. N, spec.

ftape-3.03a can be downloaded from
==================================

ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/kernel/tapes/ftape-3.03a.tar.gz
ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/kernel/tapes/ftape-3.03-3.03a.dif.gz

another possibility is to download the package from my web page:

        http://samuel.math.rwth-aachen.de/~LBFM/claus/ftape

where you can also find links to related information, old versions of
ftape and current development snapshots (if any).

Cheers

Claus

