PKG is a software packaging system developed to be compatible 
with the RPM packaging format. It allows packages to be created with a .pkg 
extension so they can be differentiated from released packages from other 
distributions, but still be installed with either pkg or rpm. PKG is 
command line identical with rpm.

You can easily build your own variant of rpm to build packages with a 
specific extension of your own choosing. Just modify the spec file and 
build away or patch the rpm source with the PKG patch and make with the 
extension you want. 

PKG is available at http://www.metrotron.com/pub/packages/pkg

PKG provides a solution to develop your own RPM compatible
packager for other distributions without using the .rpm extension (which
could be confusing). PKG also specifies a way to deal with the problem of 
handling config files (and important system binaries!) without 
removing or modifying existing important runtime config files (which
can be a serious problem on live production type systems)

For a discussion of issues and solutions see
http://www.metrotron.com/linux/release/0.90/doc/development/packagers.html
for more.

PKG is currently being used in the process of 
packaging a distribution of Linux that has been developed and
maintained at Metrotron over the last several years. 

The 'yet unnamed' distribution is available at

http://www.metrotron.com/linux/release/0.90


rpm was used as a packager base since:

* does the job 
* relatively easy to use 
* is GPLed !
* has the  package spec (and scripts) all in one file
* command line args are easy to remember 
* the code makes sense for the most part ;-)
* maintains a baseline of the installed system


I think that a more generic application of rpm (like pkg) could help
move rpm compatibility forward which would be a benefit since rpm
seems to be popular and somewhat of an ad hoc standard.

Packagers are not a religious issue with me, don't email me about that!
I just try to design things that are hopefully useful.

- --mike knerr


