    The Central Institute for Applied Mathematics at the
                 Forschungszentrum Juelich

is proud to announce the first public release of 'nassi'.

What is 'nassi' ?
- -----------------

Find out all about 'nassi' under this URL:

	http://www.kfa-juelich.de/zam/nassi

'nassi' is a tool that generates Nassi-Shneiderman diagrams under
Unix/X11 from C or PASCAL source programs or pseudocode.  For the
unaware: Nassi-Shneiderman diagrams are those nice figures, that help
you understand the control flow inside a function. Their main use is
the documentation of small or medium sized software projects.

'nassi' is designed primarily for the creation of documentation, but
may as well be used for source code analysis/browsing.  It has been
developed at the Central Institute for Applied Mathematics (ZAM) at
the Forschungszentrum Juelich and is in use there for quite a while.

For representation and postprocessing 'nassi' provides a convenient
GUI with which single diagrams can be selected, drawn on the screen,
exported in several output formats and printed.

A graphics editor allows layout changes of the whole diagram or of
single statements or control structures. Statements and whole
structures can be hidden or shifted as a block to a separate
diagram. Such changes to the layout and structure of diagrams can be
inserted into the source code via special comments and are then
available for further treatment of the source code.

'nassi' provides the option of generating output in Tgif, Xfig,
Postscript and Encapsulated Postscript.


How can you get 'nassi' ?
- -------------------------

'nassi' is distributed as shareware. Follow the above URL to get an
impression of nassi's capabilities and 'look and feel', to download an
almost full featured demo version and read all about the legal stuff.
'nassi' is available for all major UNIX platforms in binary form only.

