-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


ftape-3.03 is a floppy tape device driver for Linux.

It compiles and works with Linux-2.0.30 and Linux-2.1.36 (maybe also
with other kernels, including 1.2.13 but that is untested)

ftape-3.x developed as the reunification of the stock ftape-2.x driver
written by Bas Laarhoven and the enhancements/modifications I had
implemented in a separate development tree called "zftape".

ftape-3.03 is implemented as a stack of kernel modules:

ftape.o  - hardware driver
zftape.o - VFS frontend
sftape.o - alternate VFS frontend, resembling the old ftape-2.x
           implementation
ftape-format.o - formatting of floppy tape cartridges

ftape-3.03 fixes the following bugs (compared with ftape-3.02)
==============================================================
* when using zftape:
- - zftape.o can now handle cartridges with more than 2^31 bytes
- - "mt bsf 1" now simply moves to the beginning of the current volume
  instead of moving to the previous. 

* when using sftape:
- - sftape.o had a bug that made it impossible to read back backups
  spanning across multiple cartridges

ftape-3.03 introduces the following new features (compared with ftape-3.02)
==========================================================================
- - formatting of floppy tape cartridges
- - variable block size mode with zftape.o
- - no need to erase cartridges prior to using it with zftape
- - zftape.o now inhibits write access to the tape unless the tape is
  positioned either at BOT or EOD. It was reported that this is the
  expected behavior for a Un*x tape device.
- - compression for zftape is now implemented in a separate (optional)
  module
- - better module locking when using autoloading with kerneld


other features
==============
- - includes a modified version of the GNU mt program that can handle
  new ioctls introduced by zftape
- - installation and implementation notes in info, dvi and html format.

ftape-3.03 can be downloaded from
=================================

        ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Incoming/ftape-3.03.tar.gz

and its intended location at sunsite is

        ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/kernel/tapes/ftape-3.03.tar.gz

Another possibility is to download the package from my web page:

        http://samuel.math.rwth-aachen.de/~LBFM/claus/ftape

where you can also find links to related information, old versions of
ftape and current development snapshots (if any).

Cheers

Claus

