rlpr 1.51 is now available.  This release contains a couple of bug
fixes, mostly for rlpq.  None are crucially important. If you weren't
having any problems, there's no real need to upgrade -- however,
upgrading is still encouraged.

Briefly, rlpr uses TCP/IP to send print jobs to lpd servers anywhere
on a network.  Unlike lpr, it *does not* require that the remote
printers be explicitly known to the machine you wish to print from,
(e.g. through /etc/printcap) and thus is considerably more flexible
and requires less administration.

Most importantly, one can use rlpr to print from a machine one does
not have root access on to a machine one does.  (no, this isn't a
security hole).  There are also some additinal configurations which
are possible -- please read rlpr's README for details.

It is worth noting that one problematic area, printing to HP JetDirect
printers, has *not* been resolved yet.  Apparently, HP does not
officially support the lpd protocol, but it can be made to work. If
anyone with an HP JetDirect would like to work on this, please contact
me.

TAR.GZ
======

rlpr can be obtained from the following sources:

    Primary-site:   sunsite.unc.edu /pub/Linux/system/Printing
                    88kB rlpr-1.51.tar.gz
    Alternate-site: sherilyn.wustl.edu /pub/

*** note that sherilyn.wustl.edu will be down from 5/8 to 8/20! **

RPM
===

When the RPM's get moved out of the Incoming directory, the source RPM
and i386 binary RPM for rlpr 1.51 should be available at:

  ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/contrib/RPMS/rlpr-1.51-1.i386.rpm
  ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/contrib/SRPMS/rlpr-1.51-1.src.rpm

DEBIAN PACKAGE
==============

The Debian Project has also graciously put together an rlpr package.
The latest version can always be found at:
  ftp://www.debian.org/pub/debian/{stable,unstable}/{binary-i386,source}/net

