- -Linux Newsletter purpose & subscription statement
======================================================================= 

- -Introduction

To see information on several new products from Caldera, EST, 
Unisource, InfoMagic, Red Hat and others, see the second to the
last section in this newsletter.  

At the Linux Mall we work hard to carry every product available for Linux  
including software, books, gift items, and any other category of Linux 
product that you or we discover. If there is something you want that we 
do not now have, let us know or let the vendor of the product know and 
we'll try to get together. We already have the largest selection of 
anyone on the web, and great prices too! 

But first, the news!

- -- 
 
- -Travel to North Carolina, Superman, Linus, Penguins, and Linux Man 

I left Denver late Tuesday evening, and about noon the next day found
myself cruising through Missouri, about 100 miles beyond St. Louis
when I saw a sign that said "Metropolis, the Home of Superman". So, since
Missouri is the "Show Me" state, I thought I'd ask to meet Superman. They 
sent me about a mile down the road to the town square, which contained
a 20-foot statue of Superman.  

Speaking of Superman, Linus assures me that he is now moved in 
and settling in to work. Extremely busy, but happy with his decision 
to move to the USA.  I am glad for him.  Finally all the furniture and 
computers have arrived. 
 
Linus had told me at Disneyland that he liked the Linux Penguin OK, 
but would like to see the penguins actually doing something.  I asked 
our Linux Mall graphic artist, Bonnie Brodsky, to come up with a 
penguin in action.  If Linux does anything well, it is "RUN". 

Bonnie came up with a running penguin, and I asked Linus to take a 
gander (waterfowl pun intended) at 
http://www.LinuxMall.com/announce/disneypic.html. Along with 
the Penguin, we are thinking of using a cartoon character to be named 
LinuxMan.  He can be found at this location too.  We even have some more 
tentative graphics that include these two as well as various logos and 
buttons. If you would like to check them out and comment on them, or even 
submit alternatives,  they can be found at 
http://www.LinuxMall.com/htmlmsg/lookmark.html 
 
Well, it is time to move on from Metropolis to the destination: 
Durham, North Carolina... the home of Red Hat Software, and also the 
Linux Expo... It was a very fast run Denver to Durham from Tuesday 
night to Thursday Morning...
- --
 
- -Linux Expo, North Carolina, April 4 & 5 
 
As I was rolling down the hills that they call the Smoky Mountains, it was 
nice and clear.  I arrived within about 50 miles of Durham by 4am Thursday 
morning, so I decided it was time to get some sleep, and pulled over for a 
nap.  At 10am the following morning I called Terry Tomlinson, the 
LinuxMall rep at Red Hat, and asked her to lunch.  We had a good talk at a 
little Italian eatery, and then afterwards she gave me a tour of the Red 
Hat facilities.  They seem to be doing a good job of managing their growth.   
 
Then I went over to check out the show facilities so I would know 
what was needed when the 6pm set up time came. 
 
It turns out the Linux Mall and WorkGroup Solutions table was 
to be upstairs.  At 6pm I set up everything (this always takes 
several hours), and then left to get some much needed sleep. 
 
Friday morning came, and the show opened. People kept coming by to chat 
and to buy things.  I would have liked to have had the booth located 
downstairs so that I could have been closer to the sessions, and the main
flow of people.  That just didn't work out for this event.  A lot of people 
did come upstairs though, and I had a lot of great conversations with both 
people I already knew and people I just met.  People were raving about 
the technical sessions that they had attended. Sales were brisk for the 
Linux Mall and, I gathered, for the other vendors as well. 
 
During the show, two particular incidents occurred that stand out in my 
mind.  In one touching incident, Fred Murphy from Tech-X consulting 
several times offered to take me out to dinner during The Expo.  
Finally at the end of the show after loading the van, I actually had  
time to meet with Fred, before roaring back to Colorado.  Fred followed 
me all around while I tried to figure out where I had seen that 
"Outback Steakhouse" from the freeway. I had never been to one, but 
it sounded good to both of us right then.  Finally I gave up and Fred 
picked an Indian restaurant instead.  The food was unusual for me 
but good.  
 
Then it became clear why Fred was so insistent that we have dinner.  He 
told me that he wanted to pay for dinner, because a couple of years ago I 
had given his father a copy of Linux at the Dallas Uniforum show. This 
changed their lives. Linux opened their eyes to freedom, and gave them 
control again over their own destiny. They used Linux in building the 
information systems for their company. Fred now runs a consulting firm 
deploying Linux, and is a much appreciated Linux Mall customer, as well  
as a good influence on his community.  We at the Linux Mall are very 
grateful every day to have many wonderful customers like Fred.  

Interestingly, Fred's full name is Frederick Murphy IV, quite a 
coincidence, as my father's name was Fred, and I would be Fred 
Bolzern IV if my father had not decided to break the sequence.  

By the way, I finally saw that "Outback" on the way home.  It was at the 
Kentucky/Missouri border!  Good thing we didn't decide to hold out 
and keep looking for it. :-) 
 
Fred mentioned to me that Dr. Gregg Weinstein's talk at the Linux Expo
was a great session.  He said he would take home a number of things that 
could be used to help convince the people around him to accept Linux.
I wish that I could have gone.  I have heard him speak once before at 
UseLinux. 
 
This leads into the next incident: A fellow came by during Dr Gregg's talk 
and told me that he wanted to attend all the sessions, but this was one 
he felt he could skip because he also had something to discuss with me.  
He felt that Dr Gregg would be preaching to the choir anyway. I told 
him he *HAD* to go, and I would wait to talk to him afterwards. It would
be well worth the time, I said. He was a bit put out by my insistence, 
but went anyway.  He later came back and thanked me for insisting that 
he attend. 
 
For me, there was a lesson to be relearned from this.  It is one thing 
for me to tell someone they need Linux, and entirely another to know 
their world well enough to show them in terms they understand.  Issues 
like Return on Investment (ROI), cost of deployment and management, 
among others are important to potential commercial users. So is being 
able to do something at all if you can dream it up, and do it cost 
effectively.  Dr. Gregg humorously makes these kinds of points in a 
way that you can remember and use the next time someone asks, or needs 
information. 
 
For any of you that have a chance to attend Dr Gregg's sessions when he 
does them, I strongly recommend that you attend. As Microsoft has 
already proved, the marketing and the presentation are more important 
to the sale than technical superiority. We (the Linux community) need both. 
- -- 
 
- -Michael Cowpland, CEO Corel Corp, Java & Linux, Call for action 
 
On the subject of promoting Linux -- and I am always on the subject of 
promoting Linux! --  I think I should relate something else that happened 
to me recently.  I decided that I needed to let Michael Cowpland, the 
CEO of Corel Corp know that I was concerned that Corel would never prevail 
against Microsoft on Microsoft's own turf (OS).  So I called and left a 
message for him that said, simply: "If you wish to succeed against 
Microsoft you must change the playing field".  It seems pretty 
obvious that Microsoft software has an extreme advantage on its own 
playing field. 
 
Michael called me back and asked what I meant. I told him that Corel 
already had WordPerfect and CorelDRAW running on Linux, and needed 
to support Caldera and the Linux community as a whole with all their 
products.  It would also be good if they would make frequent mentions 
of their Linux participation to the press.  Then, he asked: "What's Linux?"  
Since Corel already sells Linux ported products, I was, needless to say, 
stunned. I got derailed having to explain what Linux was.  He didn't seem 
to have an interest in discussing "Another Operating System" at that point. 
Instead, he proceeded to tell me how the Java Suite is the future for Corel. 
 
After a brief discussion, he had to run to a meeting. Unfortunately, 
I missed an opportunity to tell him two very important benefits of Linux 
to his company: 
 
- -One: building software for Linux can happen quickly and immediately,  
 especially since both WordPerfect and CorelDRAW already run on Linux.  
 They might even be able to ship their MS Win software with a copy of 
 Linux and the Linux ports of the same software in the same box.  This 
 would help to start the trend to another playing field much faster 
 than would be possible with Java alone.  At the very least they could 
 seize every opportunity to verbally support Caldera's efforts, and 
 those of other Linux vendors, to the press.  These things would cost 
 Corel virtually nothing, and might well help to turn the tide away from 
 Microsoft's own operating systems without hurting Corel's position 
 or strategies. In fact it would plant the seeds for his real goal... 
 the cross platform, Internet deployable Java Suite. After all Linux 
 IS the number two Internet Server Platform. 
 
- -Two: Linux is one of the best possible engines to run the Java code on,  
 so by all means keep working on the Java suite... but unfortunately Java 
 is a longer term gamble than starting with Linux would be.  
 
While I agree with Java being a potential future, there are still  
technical problems to be solved in that area. Linux on the other 
hand is already solid, and could be one of the paths toward a Java 
future.  Maybe one of the many people at Corel who read this  
newsletter might have a chance to mention these benefits to Michael. 
 
Since Corel is in a very unique position to provide significant industry 
leadership at this time, anyone that you can contact at Corel that might 
help get the word out to their upper management might help.  Please leave 
the suggestion at http://www.corel.com/prodfeedback/index.htm for their 
product managers, and also hit http://www.corel.com for many  
other mailing lists and contact methods which they watch regularly.  
Please **do NOT flame** Corel, just suggest cooperation if you honestly 
feel you have positive input to offer them.

If a lot of people within Corel hear about Linux, then they might start 
to talk about it regularly.  The more they talk about it, the more 
likely they will support us.  This would be a big win for Linux 
*AND* for Corel.  

Here is a relevant communication from a Linux Newsletter reader on the
subject of Linux promotion.
