- -Reader Response, Linux story at AIIM '97 
 
Here is a report from another major trade show. Please feel free to  
mail in more such reports (Thanks John!).   
 
 
Subject: Linux story at AIIM '97 
Date: Thu, 17 Apr 1997 13:44:34 -0400 
 
I just got back from New York where I attended the AIIM '97 conference - 
Association for Information and Image Management.  The conference covered 
document management, imaging, COLD, data warehousing, etc.  As you can guess, 
nothing had to do with Linux at this show, but I do have an interesting Linux 
story none the less. 
 
On the show floor, they had set up a cyber cafe so that you can go and relax or 
check out vendors' web sites.  I took the opportunity to visit some of my 
favorite Linux sites - Linux Mall, Linux Journal, Linux Gazette, Red Hat, etc. 
Anyway, while I was surfing, a Microsoft employee wandered up and caught me. 
Apparently he "grew up" with Unix in college and had heard some things about 
Linux and was anxious to find out more - where to get it, what's available, 
etc.  So, I gave him a few web sites to get started (and sent him the HTML 
referral button to the Linux Mall this morning). 
 
I did, however, have to ask him the requisite question - "Isn't Bill going to 
slap your hands if you install Linux on your machine?"  After going through the 
requisite answer to that question - "Everybody thinks that all Microsoft 
employees are a bunch of pinhead automatons..." - he said something that 
took me by surprise.  He said that Linux has grown so much in popularity that 
Microsoft has decided to port ActiveX to Linux. 
 
This may not be news to you, but this is the first that I've heard of this.  If 
you find out any further information, it might be something worth publishing in 
your monthly news letter. 
 
John Messina, Andersen Consulting / Astra Merck Inc. 
e-mail: john.messina@astramerck.com 
