   RPMTAR -- It makes RPMs that are tar.gz files
           
     From: Jim Lowe <jhl@richmond.infi.net>

It's actually a patch to Red Hat rpm version 2.3.10, although,
since it is not endorsed by Red Hat, I am calling it `rpmtar'
for now.

It looks and acts just like Red Hat rpm except the package 
format is tar (the whole file is tar), one of the POSIX 
interchange formats and de facto internet standard for packages. 

The idea here is to decrease and unify not increase the number 
of package formats in use an thereby make it easy for application
vendors to support all distributions; and, make it easy for other
POSIX implementations to use Linux packages; and, leverage the 
great source management idea (pristine sources with patches) of 
rpm and Debian's dpkg onto a standard format.

COOL! Where do I get it?

I've aimed it at /pub/Linux/system/admin.
There are two files:
    rpmtar-2.3.10.0.bin.i386.tar.gz
    rpmtar-2.3.10.0.src.tar.gz

These are actually RPMs. You can rename them if you want, to:
    rpmtar-2.3.10.0.i386.rpm
    rpmtar-2.3.10.0.src.rpm

No Chicken-and-Egg problem here, just UN-tar them.
You may want to exclude the `.rpminfo' file with the
- --exclude option on GNU tar.

The binary distribution only contains
  bin/rpmtar
  usr/doc/rpmtar/README.tarformat
to avoid wrecking your rpm installation. You will need to
install rpm-2.3.10 or higher (configuration files are shared).


